Find a formula for the volume of the parallelepiped determined by the vector a, b, and c. 

The volume of a parallepiped is equal to the product of the area of the base and the height. 
In Figure 1.58, the base is the parallelogram determined by a and b. 
Hence, its area is ||a x b||. 
The vector a x b is perpendicular to this parallelogram; the height of the parallelepiped is ||c|| |cosΘ|, where Θ is the angle between a x b and c. 
(The absolute value is needed in case Θ > π/2)
Therefore, 

		Volume of parallelepiped = (area of base)(height)
					 = ||a x b|| ||c|| |cosΘ|
					 = |(a x b) * c|

(The appearance of the cosΘ term should alert you to the fact that dot products are lurking somewhere)

For example, the parallelepiped determined by the vectors

		a = i + 5j, b = -4i + 2j, and c = i + j + 6k

has volume equal to 

		|((i + 5j) x (-4i + 2j)) * (i + j + 6k)| = |22k * (i + j + 6k)|
							 = |22(6)|
							 = 132

The real number (a x b) * c appearing in Example 5 is known as the triple scalar product of the vectors a, b, and c. 
Since |(a x b) * c| represents the volume of the parallelepiped determined by a, b, and c, it follows immediately that 

		|(a x b) * c| = |(b x c) * a| = |(c x a) * b|,.

In fact, if you are careful with the right-hand rule, you can convince yourself that the absolute value signs are not needed; this is, 

		(a x b) * c = (b x c) * a = (c x a) * b

This is a nice example of how the geometric significance of a quantity can provide an extremely brief proof of an algebraic property the quantity must satify. 
(Try proving it by writing out the expressions in terms of components to appreciate the value of geometric insight)

